===Etymology 1===
{{etymon|en|:inh|enm:yow}}
From {{inh|en|enm|you}}, {{m|enm|yow}}, {{m|enm|ȝow|pos=object case of {{m|enm|ye}}}}, from {{inh|en|ang|ēow|t=you|pos=dative case of {{m|ang|ġē}}}}, from {{inh|en|gmw-pro|*iwwi|t=you|pos=dative case of {{m|gmw-pro|*jiʀ}}}}, from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*iwwiz|t=you|pos=dative case of {{m|gem-pro|*jīz}}}}, the Western form of {{inh|en|gem-pro|*izwiz|t=you|pos=dative case of {{m|gem-pro|*jūz}}}}, from {{der|en|ine-pro|*yúHs|t=you|pos=plural}}.

Cognate with {{cog|sco|you|t=you}}, {{cog|stq|jou|t=you}}, {{cog|fy|jo|t=you}}, {{cog|nds|jo}}, {{m|nds|joe}} and {{m|nds|oe|t=you}}, {{cog|nl|jou}} and {{m|nl|u|t=you}}, {{cog|de|euch|t=you}}, {{cog|gmh|eu}}, {{m|gmh|iu|t=you|pos=object pronoun}}, {{cog|la|vōs|t=you}}, {{cog|ae|𐬬𐬋|t=you}}, {{cog|ask|yë̃|t=you}}, {{cog|bsh|šo|t=you}}, {{cog|sa|यूयम्|tr=yūyám|t=you}}.

See usage notes. {{m|en|ye|Ye}}, {{m|en|you}} and {{m|en|your}} are cognate with {{cog|nl|jij}}/{{m|nl|je}}, {{m|nl|jou}}, {{m|nl|jouw}}; {{cog|nds|ji}}, {{m|nds|jo}}/{{m|nds|ju}}, {{m|nds|jug}} and {{cog|de|ihr}}, {{m|de|euch}} and {{m|de|euer}} respectively. {{m|en|ye|Ye}} is also cognate with {{cog|da|I}} and archaic {{cog|sv|I}}. Compare the shift to the second-person singular informal Spanish {{l|es|vos}}, historically a formal pronoun.

